Scott recalls that \(D\trianglelefteq D+D\) for any system and asks whether \(D\trianglelefteq D\times D\) and \(D\trianglelefteq D\to D\), whether those projection pairs are computable when \(D\) is effectively given, and whether more than one pair exists in each case. `exercise_8_20` is the conjunction of `D_trianglelefteq_prod` and `D_trianglelefteq_funSpace`, for an arbitrary neighbourhood system. The product retract specializes `trianglelefteq_prod_fst`: `embedFst` sends \(a\) to \(\langle a,\bot\rangle\) and the first projection retracts, with `proju2080_comp_embedFst` the identity because the first component of that pair is \(a\), and `embedFst_comp_proju2080_le` the inequality because the composite returns \(\langle z_0,\bot\rangle\), which lies under \(z\) since bottom lies under the second component. The symmetric pair is `trianglelefteq_prod_snd`, with `embedSnd` sending \(b\) to \(\langle\bot,b\rangle\), `proju2081_comp_embedSnd` the identity, and `embedSnd_comp_proju2081_le` the inequality. When \(D\) has an element distinct from bottom, `embedFst_ne_embedSnd_of_ne_bot` separates the two embeddings by comparing first components at that element, so the product carries two projection pairs. The function-space retract specializes `trianglelefteq_funSpace_const`: `constFunMap` is the curry of the first projection of \(B\times A\), sending \(b\) to the constant function of value \(b\), and `evalBotMap` is evaluation after pairing the identity with the constant map at bottom. `evalBotMap_comp_constFunMap` is the identity, because the constant function of value \(b\) returns \(b\) at bottom (`toElementMap_evalBotMap`), and `constFunMap_comp_evalBotMap_le` lies under the identity because the constant function of value \(\varphi(\bot)\), evaluated at \(a\), equals \(\varphi(\bot)\), which lies under \(\varphi(a)\) by monotonicity. Computability of either pair for an effectively given \(D\) is not a theorem of this closure, and a second projection pair for \(D\to D\) is not constructed. The background retract \(D\trianglelefteq D+D\) is left as already known. **Theorem:** partial — both retracts hold for every \(D\), and a second product pair is proved when \(D\) has an element above bottom; computability, and a second function-space pair, are not theorems. **Proof:** yes — the product is pairing with bottom and projecting, and the function space is the constant-function embedding with evaluation at bottom.
